* fixup: address PR #169 adversarial review (HIGH cursor race + HIGH messaging LIMIT + MEDIUM subscribe snapshot + MEDIUM batched markRead)

HIGH — wrap channel fetch+advance in db.transaction (lib/attention.ts):
  Two concurrent compose() calls for the same (agentId, channel) could both
  read cursor=N, both fetch the same messages, and both think they marked
  them seen. Now each channel's fetch + cursor advance is one txn that
  re-reads the cursor inside the txn boundary; the forward-only
  'cursor < maxId' guard handles the resulting conflict cleanly.

HIGH — parameterize messaging.getAfter LIMIT (lib/messaging.ts):
  Previously hardcoded LIMIT 200, ignoring the caller's limit option on the
  cursor-based read path. attention.compose() advertises CHANNEL_FETCH_LIMIT
  = 100 but was silently getting up to 200. Now LIMIT ? bound, with poll()
  updated to pass 1 (it only needs the next message anyway).

MEDIUM — snapshot subscribe cursor at channel's current max (lib/attention.ts):
  Previously subscribe inserted cursor=0, which on a long-running channel
  meant a new subscriber's first compose() drained CHANNEL_FETCH_LIMIT
  messages and orphaned everything before that. Now snapshot-isolated:
  subscribe captures MAX(id) at subscribe time, so new subscribers see only
  forward messages. Added test 'subscribe snapshots channel state' to lock
  the semantic.

MEDIUM — batched inbox markRead via db.transaction (lib/attention.ts):
  Previously N synchronous UPDATE calls in a JS for-loop (up to 500 at the
  max limit, ~50ms stall). Now one db.transaction wrapping the per-message
  markRead calls so it's one fsync regardless of N. Per-message (NOT
  markAllRead) preserved so messages beyond the fetch limit stay unread.

LOW — document limit clamp asymmetry (lib/attention.ts):
  Route rejects limit<=0 with 400 before reaching the lib. Lib still clamps
  defensively for direct callers. Added comment explaining why both layers
  exist and what they cover.

